  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nRight now a shortwave is moving from SW to NE out of the Bighorn\nBasin into MT. As the morning goes on, moisture will continue to\nbe advected into our CWA by ESE winds out of the Great Plains \nwhere dew points are in the 50s to 60s. This increased moisture \nwill allow for some embedded cells to produce locally heavy \nrainfall through noon, especially east of Billings. While there \nis not much CAPE around the area at the moment, there is between \n250-1000 J/Kg locally across the entire CWA. Also with the \npreviously mentioned ESE winds, this will also be advecting in \nhigher theta-e values. All that to say that lightning remains a \npossibility with any and all precip through the day today, even \nbefore sunrise. The atmosphere near and west/southwest of \nBillings is a little drier at the surface which would allow for \nsome breezy winds under these showers. There has already been \ngusts in the 30s at some sites and this will continue. No severe \nwinds are expected. As the newly developed surface low exits east-centeral\nMontana into ND this afternoon, CAMs show a flare up in \nconvection just ahead of the low. There is varying degrees of just\nhow strong a storm or two could get and just how fast it\nintensifies but it does appear possible. This will likely depend \non how much theta-e advection there is, overall CAPE values, and \nhow fast the low can spin up (wind shear). It does appear that if\nwe can get a strong storm going it will not be in our forecast \narea very long before exiting into ND. Those in Custer and Fallon \ncounties should remain weather aware this afternoon for this\npotential.\n\nDue to today's feature being of sub-tropical origin, there will \nbe very little temperature cool down behind it. Highs in the upper\n70s and low 80s will remain across the CWA through Friday. \n\nLate Friday into early Saturday, we will see two systems move into\nour area. The shortwave trough moving in along the polar jet, and\nthen a weaker vort max moving almost due north along the Rocky \nMountain Front. The latter feature seemed like it would actually \nbring more impacts to our CWA than the former. This is changing\nslightly with the most recent model runs. This small low is the \nsystem that is currently impacting portions of NM and dumping \nlarge amounts of rain. While it will be modified and not purely \nsub-tropical by the time it reaches eastern WY/MT, it will still \nbe carrying with it a good amount of moisture. This could give \nareas east of Billings a chance to get a quick dose of rain headed\ninto the weekend, although the most recent runs show nearly all of\nthe moisture staying to the east of our CWA. The main shortwave \nmoving out of the Pacific could be a wind maker for some places. \nAs of right now, nearly all models have the weak feature from NM shoving\nthe shortwave just poleward enough to keep the 700mb jet into the\nTFX and GGW CWAs. If this does not happen though, or is not \nshoved poleward as much, Saturday could be a windy day across the \nregion. Even if the less windy scenario plays out, gusts in the \n30s mph will probably occur in the west gap wind prone locations \n(Big Timber, Harlowton). Be sure to check future forecasts if you\nhave plans for the first half of the weekend.\n\nBoth the ECMWF and the GFS keep our area in a progressive pattern\nthrough the first part of next week with several shortwaves coming\noff of the Pacific and moving through Montana. The main question \nfor next week's forecast will be what happens with a handful of \ntropical systems. The first is former Typhoon Dujuan which is now\nin the polar jet stream. There could also be a few major \nhurricanes that form in the Eastern Pacific over the next week \nwhich could alter the tropical branch of the jet and/or advect a \nlot of moisture into the mid-latitudes. WMR",
